    <string-array name="x_fotd_facts">
        <item>The first Android phone, the T-Mobile G1 (HTC Dream), had a slide-out keyboard and a trackball.</item>
        <item>Cupcake (1.5) was the first Android with an on-screen keyboard and home screen widgets.</item>
        <item>Donut (1.6) taught Android to handle different screen sizes and added the quick search box.</item>
        <item>Eclair (2.0–2.1) brought live wallpapers and free turn-by-turn Google Maps Navigation.</item>
        <item>Froyo (2.2) turned phones into Wi-Fi hotspots and added a JIT compiler that made apps much faster.</item>
        <item>Gingerbread (2.3) added NFC and support for more than one camera, like front cameras.</item>
        <item>Honeycomb (3.x) was made only for tablets and introduced the blue \"Holo\" look.</item>
        <item>Ice Cream Sandwich (4.0) put the Back, Home and Recents buttons on the screen and added Face Unlock.</item>
        <item>Jelly Bean (4.1) brought \"Project Butter\" for smooth 60 fps scrolling, and Google Now.</item>
        <item>KitKat (4.4) was a real deal with Nestlé, and it was tuned to run on phones with only 512 MB of RAM.</item>
        <item>Lollipop (5.0) introduced Material Design and replaced the Dalvik runtime with ART.</item>
        <item>Marshmallow (6.0) let you allow or deny app permissions while using the app, and added fingerprint support.</item>
        <item>Nougat (7.0) brought split-screen, two apps side by side, to phones.</item>
        <item>Oreo (8.0) added picture-in-picture and notification dots on app icons.</item>
        <item>Pie (9) introduced gesture navigation with a pill-shaped Home button, and Digital Wellbeing.</item>
        <item>Android 10 stopped using dessert names in public and added a dark theme for the whole system.</item>
    </string-array>
